Abstract 

We present a mathematically grounded framework for a class of synthetic cognitive systems, the Universal Logic Matrix (ULM). The ULM is defined as a category whose objects are self‑contained logic modules (capsules) and whose morphisms are communication channels. The architecture rests on four rigorously formulated pillars: (i) fractal self‑similarity via iterated function systems (IFS) whose attractors guarantee computable Hausdorff dimension; (ii) thermodynamic cost functions rooted in Landauer’s principle and derivation entropy; (iii) integrated information theory (IIT) expressed through the system integrated information \varphi_s and a local mechanism measure \varphi; and (iv) self‑healing and evolution formalised through categorical pushouts, sheaf cohomology, and symmetries of the system’s action functional. Every core claim is stated as a theorem, lemma, or conjecture with explicit proofs or dependencies. The thesis thereby transforms the ULM from a metaphorical vision into a body of precise, testable mathematics.

