A.1 Metric Spaces and Fractal Geometry

Definition A.1 (Metric Space).
A metric space (X,d) is a set X together with a function d:X\times X\to\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} satisfying, for all x,y,z\in X:

1. d(x,y)=0 iff x=y;
2. d(x,y)=d(y,x);
3. d(x,y)\le d(x,z)+d(z,y).

Definition A.2 (Contraction Mapping).
A map \phi:X\to X is a contraction if there exists a constant 0\le r<1 such that

d(\phi(x),\phi(y))\le r\,d(x,y)\qquad\text{for all }x,y\in X.

The infimum of such r is the contraction ratio.

Definition A.3 (Iterated Function System).
An iterated function system (IFS) on a complete metric space (X,d) is a finite family \{\phi_i\}_{i=1}^N of contractions. The attractor of the IFS is the unique non‑empty compact set F\subset X satisfying the invariance equation

F = \bigcup_{i=1}^N \phi_i(F).

Definition A.4 (Hausdorff Dimension).
For a set A\subset X, the s‑dimensional Hausdorff measure is

\mathcal{H}^s(A) = \lim_{\delta\to 0} \inf\left\{ \sum_{j=1}^\infty (\operatorname{diam} U_j)^s : A\subset\bigcup_j U_j,\; \operatorname{diam} U_j\le \delta \right\}.

The Hausdorff dimension of A is

\dim_H(A) = \inf\{ s\ge 0 : \mathcal{H}^s(A)=0 \} = \sup\{ s\ge 0 : \mathcal{H}^s(A)=\infty \}.

Definition A.5 (Similarity Dimension).
If the IFS consists of similitudes (maps that scale all distances by a factor r_i), the similarity dimension s is the unique real solution of

\sum_{i=1}^N r_i^{\,s} = 1.

Definition A.6 (Open Set Condition).
An IFS \{\phi_i\} satisfies the Open Set Condition (OSC) if there exists a non‑empty bounded open set V\subset X such that \phi_i(V)\subset V for all i and \phi_i(V)\cap\phi_j(V)=\emptyset for i\neq j.

Theorem A.1 (Moran–Hutchinson).
If an IFS of similitudes satisfies the OSC, then the Hausdorff dimension of the attractor equals the similarity dimension.

Proof. See Hutchinson (1981); Falconer (2003). ∎

