A.2 Information Theory and Thermodynamics

Definition A.7 (Shannon Entropy).
For a discrete random variable X with probability mass function p(x), the Shannon entropy is

H(X) = -\sum_{x} p(x)\log_2 p(x)\quad\text{bits}.

Landauer’s Principle.
Erasure of one bit of information in a physical system at absolute temperature T dissipates at least k_B T\ln 2 joules into the environment, where k_B is Boltzmann’s constant. Generalised forms (Sagawa & Ueda, 2009) relate the bound to the algorithmic complexity of the erased data.

Definition A.8 (Derivation Entropy – Simplified).
For a computation that produces an output from an input, let the set of all computational paths be \mathcal{P}. For each path p let B(p) be the total number of bits irreversibly erased. The derivation entropy is

S_{\text{der}} = k_B T\ln 2 \cdot \min_{p\in\mathcal{P}} B(p).

