A.3 Integrated Information Theory (IIT)

We adopt the system integrated information formalism of Marshall et al. (2022).

Definition A.9 (Discrete Dynamical System).
Consider a system of n binary elements with state vector X^t\in\{0,1\}^n and a transition probability function

P: \{0,1\}^n \to \{0,1\}^n,\qquad P_{x,y} = \mathbb{P}(X^{t+1}=y\mid X^t=x).

Definition A.10 (System Integrated Information \varphi_s).
Let \pi be a partition of the system into k independent parts. The partitioned transition matrix P^\pi is the Kronecker product of the marginal transition matrices. Then

\varphi_s = \min_{\pi} D_{\mathrm{II}}(P\,\|\,P^\pi),

where D_{\mathrm{II}} is the intrinsic difference measure, a variant of the Kullback–Leibler divergence adapted to the system’s state‑dependent cause–effect structure. For practical purposes we use the KL divergence evaluated at the stationary distribution:

\varphi_s^{\mathrm{KL}} = \min_{\pi} \sum_{x} \mu(x) \sum_{y} P_{x,y} \log \frac{P_{x,y}}{P^\pi_{x,y}},

with \mu the stationary measure of P.

Definition A.11 (Mechanism Integrated Information \varphi(m)).
For a mechanism m (a subset of system elements) within a larger complex, \varphi(m) is the \varphi_s of the smallest subsystem that contains m as an irreducible component. If the full system is the complex, one may approximate \varphi(m) by computing \varphi_s for the subgraph consisting of m and its Markov blanket.

