A.4 Category Theory

Definition A.12 (Category).
A category \mathbf{C} consists of a collection of objects \mathrm{Ob}(\mathbf{C}) and for each pair A,B a set of morphisms \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{C}}(A,B), equipped with an associative composition and identity morphisms.

Definition A.13 (Functor).
A functor F:\mathbf{C}\to\mathbf{D} assigns to each object A an object F(A) and to each morphism f:A\to B a morphism F(f):F(A)\to F(B), preserving composition and identities.

Definition A.14 (Pushout).
Given a span A\xleftarrow{f} C \xrightarrow{g} B, a pushout is an object P with morphisms a:A\to P, b:B\to P such that a\circ f = b\circ g, and for any other such (Q, a', b') there is a unique u:P\to Q with u\circ a = a' and u\circ b = b'.

Definition A.15 (Polynomial Endofunctor).
On the category of sets, a polynomial functor is

F(X) = \sum_{b\in B} X^{E(b)},

where B is a set of shapes and E(b) is a set of positions. Its initial algebra is the set of well‑founded trees.

---

A.5 Sheaf Theory on Graphs

Definition A.16 (Cellular Sheaf).
A cellular sheaf \mathcal{F} on a graph G=(V,E) assigns:

· to each vertex v a vector space \mathcal{F}(v),
· to each edge e:u\to v a vector space \mathcal{F}(e),
· linear restriction maps \mathcal{F}_{u\trianglelefteq e}:\mathcal{F}(u)\to \mathcal{F}(e) and \mathcal{F}_{v\trianglelefteq e}:\mathcal{F}(v)\to \mathcal{F}(e).

A global section is a choice of s_v\in\mathcal{F}(v) such that for every edge e:u\to v, \mathcal{F}_{u\trianglelefteq e}(s_u) = \mathcal{F}_{v\trianglelefteq e}(s_v).

Definition A.17 (Sheaf Laplacian).
The sheaf Laplacian L_\mathcal{F}: C^0(G;\mathcal{F})\to C^0(G;\mathcal{F}) is defined, for s\in C^0, by

(L_\mathcal{F} s)_v = \sum_{e: u\to v} \mathcal{F}_{v\trianglelefteq e}^* (\mathcal{F}_{v\trianglelefteq e} s_v - \mathcal{F}_{u\trianglelefteq e} s_u).

The quadratic form s^\top L_\mathcal{F} s measures the total inconsistency; it is zero precisely on global sections.

