Appendix B: Fractal Capsule Expansion – Proofs

We provide full details for the fractal dimension theorem of the ULM.

Definition B.1 (Capsule Configuration Space).
Let \mathcal{S} be the signature space, a metric space ( \mathcal{S}, d_{\mathcal{S}}). The configuration space \mathcal{C} is the set of all finite multisets of capsules, each capsule being a tuple (S_\kappa, \Phi_\kappa, \Sigma_\kappa, E_\kappa). For two configurations X=\{\kappa_i\}, Y=\{\kappa'_j\}, define the Hausdorff distance

d_{\mathcal{C}}(X,Y) = \max\Bigl\{ \sup_i \inf_j d_{\mathcal{S}}(\Sigma_{\kappa_i},\Sigma_{\kappa'_j}),\;
\sup_j \inf_i d_{\mathcal{S}}(\Sigma_{\kappa'_j},\Sigma_{\kappa_i}) \Bigr\}.

Lemma B.1. (\mathcal{C}, d_{\mathcal{C}}) is a complete metric space.

Proof. The space of finite subsets of a compact metric space with the Hausdorff distance is complete when considering closed subsets. The set of signatures is a compact subset of \mathcal{S} (assuming a finite bound). Adding the multiset structure with identical capsules distinguished by an arbitrary ordering does not affect completeness because the Hausdorff metric disregards multiplicities. ∎

Definition B.2 (Spawning IFS).
Fix a finite set of capsule types K. For each type \kappa\in K, define a map \phi_\kappa:\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{C} by: if a configuration X contains no capsule of type \kappa with signature exactly \Sigma_\kappa, then \phi_\kappa(X)=X. If X contains at least one such capsule, then replace one occurrence with the multiset

\{\kappa, \kappa^{(1)},\dots,\kappa^{(n_\kappa)}\},

where the offspring capsules \kappa^{(i)} are identical in structure except their signature is transformed by a fixed contraction h_\kappa:\mathcal{S}\to\mathcal{S} that satisfies d_{\mathcal{S}}(h_\kappa(a), h_\kappa(b)) = r_\kappa\, d_{\mathcal{S}}(a,b) for some r_\kappa\in(0,1), and their state space is scaled by the same factor r_\kappa. We also assume the offspring signatures are distinct from the parent’s signature in the sense that the distance between any two contracted signatures is positive if the original signatures were distinct and separated.

Lemma B.2. Each \phi_\kappa is a contraction on (\mathcal{C}, d_{\mathcal{C}}) with ratio r_\kappa.

Proof. Take two configurations X,Y. If neither contains a capsule of type \kappa eligible for spawning, the map is the identity and trivially a contraction. Otherwise, the operation alters the configuration by replacing one capsule with several. Because all distances between signatures scale by r_\kappa, the Hausdorff distance after transformation is bounded by r_\kappa times the original distance. ∎

Lemma B.3 (Open Set Condition).
Assume the family of contraction maps h_\kappa on \mathcal{S} is such that for different capsule types \kappa\neq\kappa', the sets of reachable signatures are separated: there exists \delta>0 such that for any two signatures \sigma,\sigma' arising from different type spawns, d_{\mathcal{S}}(\sigma,\sigma')\ge \delta. Then the IFS \{\phi_\kappa\}_{\kappa\in K} satisfies the OSC.

Proof. Let V\subset\mathcal{C} be the open set of configurations where every pairwise signature distance is at least \delta/2. For any X\in V, applying \phi_\kappa contracts all distances by r_\kappa, preserving the separation. Moreover, for \kappa\neq\kappa', the images \phi_\kappa(V) and \phi_{\kappa'}(V) consist of configurations whose signatures are separated by at least \delta/2 by assumption, hence they are disjoint. ∎

Theorem B.1 (Fractal Dimension of ULM Attractor).
Under the conditions of Lemma B.3, the attractor F\subset\mathcal{C} of the spawning IFS has Hausdorff dimension s given by the unique solution of

\sum_{\kappa\in K} r_\kappa^{\,s} = 1.

If all capsules spawn n offspring with identical ratio r, then \dim_H(F) = \frac{\log n}{-\log r}.

Proof. By Lemma B.2, each \phi_\kappa is a similitude with ratio r_\kappa. Lemma B.3 establishes the OSC. Theorem A.1 (Moran–Hutchinson) then implies \dim_H(F) = s, where s solves the equation. The special case follows immediately. ∎

