Appendix C: Thermodynamic Cost and Derivation Entropy

Definition C.1 (Irreversible Bit Erasure per Capsule).
For a capsule \kappa performing a forward pass, let b(\kappa) be the number of bits that are discarded (overwritten without being read elsewhere). This can be computed from the circuit complexity of the capsule’s logical operations: any logical operation that loses information (e.g., AND gate) erases one bit per input combination where the output is not invertible. The minimal Landauer cost for one activation is

C(\kappa) = k_B T \ln 2 \cdot b(\kappa).

Definition C.2 (Derivation Entropy of a Capsule Network).
Let a computational task be specified by an input state \mathbf{x} and desired output \mathbf{y}. The network of capsules performs a series of transformations. Let \Gamma be the directed acyclic graph of data flow. A computational path p is a complete chain of operations from input to output. For each such path, the total bits erased is the sum of b(\kappa) over all capsules activated along the path (accounting for parallel branches). The derivation entropy is

S_{\text{der}}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}) = k_B T\ln 2 \cdot \min_{p\text{ realising } \mathbf{x}\mapsto\mathbf{y}} \sum_{\kappa\in p} b(\kappa).

The global energy budget constraint for a single task cycle is

S_{\text{der}} \le E_{\text{total}} = \sum_{\kappa\in\text{active}} E_\kappa.

Proposition C.1 (Thermodynamic Bound).
For any computation performed by the ULM, the total heat dissipated Q satisfies

Q \ge S_{\text{der}}.

Moreover, if the system operates reversibly wherever possible, the bound can be approached arbitrarily closely.

Proof. Each irreversible bit erasure dissipates at least k_B T\ln 2. Summing over all erased bits along the minimal‑cost path gives the claimed bound. The achievability follows from Bennett’s reversible computing analysis. ∎

