Appendix D: Integrated Information and the Φ‑Threshold Gate

Definition D.1 (Capsule Sub‑system for φ).
For a capsule \kappa, define its Markov blanket B(\kappa) as the set of capsules that are directly connected to \kappa via \Delta-morphisms (the communication channels). Form the joint system X = (\kappa, B(\kappa)) with a stationary joint distribution obtained from the transition dynamics (detailed in Appendix F). The mechanism integrated information \varphi(\kappa) is the system integrated information \varphi_s of this joint system computed using the KL‑based approximation:

\varphi(\kappa) = \min_{\pi} D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left( P_{X} \,\|\, P_{X}^\pi \right),

where the minimisation is over all partitions that separate \kappa from any subset of B(\kappa), and the divergence is taken at the stationary measure.

Remark. Computing exact \varphi_s is #P‑hard; for neighbourhoods with up to 10 capsules we can compute it exactly. For larger neighbourhoods, we employ the practical approximation of Toker & Sommer (2022).

Lemma D.1 (Monotonicity of φ under Integration).
If two capsules \kappa_1,\kappa_2 are merged into a single module, the resulting φ of the merged module is at least as large as the maximum of the individual φ values, provided the partition that gave the minimum for each is still considered.

Proof. Because the set of partitions increases, the minimum over a larger set cannot be greater. ∎

Definition D.2 (Φ‑Threshold Gate).
The activation status function \Omega assigns:

\Omega(\kappa) =
\begin{cases}
\text{active}, & \text{if } \varphi(\kappa) \ge \Phi_{\min} \text{ and } C(\kappa) \le E_\kappa,\\
\text{dormant}, & \text{if } \varphi(\kappa) \ge \Phi_{\min} \text{ but } C(\kappa) > E_\kappa,\\
\text{mutating}, & \text{if } \varphi(\kappa) < \Phi_{\min}.
\end{cases}

