Appendix E: Categorical Formalization and Self‑Healing

E.1 The ULM Category

Definition E.1 (ULM Category \mathcal{U}).

· Objects: all capsules \kappa (as defined in Chapter 2).
· Morphisms: a morphism f:\kappa\to\kappa' is a pair (T_f, w_f) where T_f: S_\kappa\to S_{\kappa'} is a linear map and w_f\in\mathbb{R}_+ is a weight (conductance).
· Composition: (T_g, w_g)\circ (T_f, w_f) = (T_g\circ T_f, w_g\cdot w_f).
· Identity: (\mathrm{id}_{S_\kappa}, 1).

Definition E.2 (Causal DAG).
The directed acyclic causal graph G=(V,E) has V=\mathrm{Ob}(\mathcal{U}) and an edge \kappa\to\kappa' if there exists a morphism with w_f > \theta_c (a threshold) and the dynamics respect temporal ordering.

E.2 Pushout Self‑Healing

Theorem E.1 (Pushout Replacement Correctness).
Given a faulty capsule \kappa_f and a dormant backup capsule \kappa_b with identical signature and state space, let there be a linear isomorphism \phi: S_{\kappa_f}\to S_{\kappa_b} (the copy map). Consider the span of sub‑ULMs:

\mathcal{K} \xleftarrow{\iota} \{\kappa_f\} \xrightarrow{\phi} \{\kappa_b\},

where \mathcal{K} is the full ULM with \kappa_f removed (i.e., the context). The pushout of this span in the category of ULM subcategories (with inclusion functors) yields a ULM in which \kappa_b replaces \kappa_f while preserving all connections: any morphism f:\kappa_f\to \kappa' becomes a morphism f\circ \phi^{-1}:\kappa_b\to\kappa', and similarly for incoming morphisms.

Proof. Construct the pushout object as the union of \mathcal{K} and \{\kappa_b\} with the identification \iota(\kappa_f) \sim \phi(\kappa_f). The universal property ensures a unique extension of all morphisms. The linear isomorphism guarantees that the state of \kappa_b can be set to the copied state without loss of information. ∎

E.3 Sheaf Consistency

Definition E.3 (Capsule Sheaf).
On the causal graph G, define a sheaf \mathcal{F} by:

· \mathcal{F}(\kappa) = S_\kappa (state space).
· For each channel e:\kappa\to\kappa', \mathcal{F}(e) = \operatorname{Im}(T_f) where T_f is the morphism’s linear map; restriction maps \mathcal{F}_{\kappa\trianglelefteq e}: S_\kappa \to \operatorname{Im}(T_f) is the projection onto that subspace; \mathcal{F}_{\kappa'\trianglelefteq e}: S_{\kappa'} \to \operatorname{Im}(T_f) is T_f composed with the appropriate projection.

Theorem E.2 (Sheaf Laplacian Minimisation).
The consistency energy E_{\text{sheaf}}(s) = \frac{1}{2}\|L_\mathcal{F}^{1/2} s\|^2 is convex and its global minima are exactly the global sections of the sheaf. Gradient descent on E_{\text{sheaf}} drives any initial state exponentially fast to the space of global sections.

Proof. The sheaf Laplacian is symmetric positive‑semidefinite. Its kernel consists of global sections. The quadratic form is strictly convex when restricted to the orthogonal complement of the kernel, guaranteeing exponential convergence. ∎

