Appendix F: Dynamical System and Lyapunov Stability

Definition F.1 (ULM State Equation).
For each capsule \kappa, the continuous‑time evolution of its state vector x_\kappa is

\frac{dx_\kappa}{dt} = -\nabla_{x_\kappa} \mathcal{E}(x) + \sigma \xi_\kappa(t),

where \xi_\kappa is standard white Gaussian noise, and the total potential \mathcal{E} is

\mathcal{E}(x) = \alpha S_{\text{der}}(x) + \beta E_{\text{sheaf}}(x) - \gamma \Phi_{\text{total}}(x),

with \alpha,\beta,\gamma>0. S_{\text{der}} is computed as the minimal derivation entropy over current active paths; E_{\text{sheaf}} is the sheaf inconsistency energy; \Phi_{\text{total}} = \sum_{\kappa\text{ active}} \varphi(\kappa).

Theorem F.1 (Lyapunov Stability).
Assume \sigma=0 and the cost terms S_{\text{der}} and E_{\text{sheaf}} are continuously differentiable and convex, while -\Phi_{\text{total}} is convex (or has bounded second derivative). Then \mathcal{E} is a Lyapunov function for the deterministic dynamics, i.e., d\mathcal{E}/dt \le 0 along trajectories, with equality only at critical points of \mathcal{E}. Consequently, the system converges to the set of local minima of \mathcal{E}.

Proof. Direct computation:

\frac{d\mathcal{E}}{dt} = \sum_\kappa \nabla_{x_\kappa}\mathcal{E} \cdot \frac{dx_\kappa}{dt}
= -\sum_\kappa \|\nabla_{x_\kappa}\mathcal{E}\|^2 \le 0.

Equality holds exactly when \nabla_{x_\kappa}\mathcal{E}=0 for all \kappa. The function is bounded below (it is a sum of non‑negative terms minus a bounded positive term), so trajectories converge to the critical set. ∎

