Appendix G: Symmetry and Noether Invariants

Theorem G.1 (Noether Charges in the ULM).
Consider the action functional

\mathcal{A} = \int_0^\tau \left( \sum_\kappa \frac{1}{2}m_\kappa \|\dot{x}_\kappa\|^2 - \mathcal{E}(x) \right) dt.

If the potential \mathcal{E} is invariant under a continuous group G of transformations of the state variables x_\kappa (e.g., x_\kappa \mapsto U x_\kappa for a fixed orthogonal matrix U), then the corresponding Noether charge

Q = \sum_\kappa m_\kappa \dot{x}_\kappa \cdot \delta x_\kappa

is conserved along solutions of the Euler–Lagrange equations.

Proof. Apply the standard Noether theorem (Goldstein, 2002) to the Lagrangian L = \sum_\kappa \frac{1}{2}m_\kappa \|\dot{x}_\kappa\|^2 - \mathcal{E}(x). The invariance condition \delta L = 0 yields a conserved current, which for point particles reduces to the given Q. ∎

Example. If the ULM’s energy functional is invariant under permutations of capsule indices (i.e., all capsules of a given type are interchangeable), then the total “capsule index” momentum is conserved, preventing unbounded preferential growth of any single capsule.

